[117.844162, "o", "  [tools] 12 call(s) without changing a file — nudging to decide\r\n  deepseek-v4.1-flash — 12 tool call(s) so far\r\nYour pip isn't misconfigured — it's **half-deleted**. The `pip` package source is gone from site-packages while its metadata remains, so the `pip` launcher script dies on import.\r\n\r\n**Evidence (read off the machine, not inferred):**\r\n\r\n- `/usr/local/bin/pip` runs under `#!/usr/local/bin/python3.13` — the same interpreter as `python3`, so the usual \"pip belongs to a different python\" trap is *not* your problem.\r\n- That interpreter's site-packages contains `pip-24.3.1.dist-info` but **no `pip/` directory**. `pip-24.3.1.dist-info` is exactly why the repair tools will tell you pip is \"already satisfied\" while every `pip install` fails.\r\n- The failure text is a traceback, not a policy refusal:\r\n\r\n```\r\nFile \"/usr/local/bin/pip\", line 5, in <module>\r\n    from pip._internal.cli.main import main\r\nModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'pip'\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Ruled out, so you don't waste time on them:** PEP "]
[117.844206, "o", "668 externally-managed (that marker exists only for the OS Python 3.11 in `/usr/lib`; your active Python is 3.13 in `/usr/local`, which has none), permissions (you're uid 0 and own site-packages), disk space (~145 GB free), SSL (OpenSSL 3.0.22 working), and pip.conf overrides (none on the box).\r\n\r\n**Fix — run these yourself; I didn't touch the installation:**\r\n\r\n```sh\r\n# Definitive: rebuild pip from the official bootstrap\r\ncurl -sS https://bootstrap.pypa.io/get-pip.py -o get-pip.py\r\npython3 get-pip.py\r\nrm get-pip.py\r\npip --version && pip install --dry-run requests   # both should now succeed\r\n```\r\n\r\nIf `python3 -m ensurepip --upgrade` first says \"Requirement already satisfied\", that's the stale `dist-info` talking — remove `/usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/pip-24.3.1.dist-info` (and any `pip-*.dist-info` beside it) and re-run `ensurepip`, or just use `get-pip.py` above.\r\n\r\nOne caveat, since you said \"system-wide\": this box has two Pythons — 3.13 in `/usr/local` (the one `python3`/`pip` resolve to"]
[117.844269, "o", ") and the distro's 3.11. If you actually meant the distro one, install its pip through the OS (`apt-get install python3-pip`) instead; the commands above repair 3.13 only.\r\n\r\nTwo things I did not do: I ran no repair (a diagnostic turn shouldn't mutate a system interpreter), and the raw command output above came from a delegated shell turn rather than my own tools, which have no `bash`.\r\n\r\n[result] role=coordinator model=deepseek-v4.1-flash tools=12 surface=c833e5a9bf75\r\n"]
